Postdoc:  ```PLAN
Title: Ensemble-Based Bayesian Aggregation with Uncertainty-Guided Clarifications for Multiturn Human-LLM Collaboration

Overview:
We propose an innovative experiment to maximize accuracy in multiturn collaborations by blending an ensemble of Monte Carlo-based reward predictors with Bayesian meta-calibration and an uncertainty-triggered clarification module. The system will be applied to three domains: document editing (MediumDocEdit-Chat), code generation (BigCodeBench-Chat), and math problem solving (MATH-Chat).

Components:
1. Ensemble Reward Predictors:
   • Build multiple reward predictors, each running Monte Carlo sampling with different configurations (e.g., window sizes w ∈ {1, 2, 3} and sample sizes S ∈ {3, 5}).
   • Each predictor estimates conversation-level reward R*(t|g) = Rext(t, g) + Rint(t), where:
       – Rext(t, g): Task-specific success metrics (e.g., BLEU + narrative coherence for document editing; unit test pass rate and static analysis for code generation; accuracy + logical coherence for math tasks).
       – Rint(t): Intrinsic reward incorporating an efficiency penalty (−min[λ · TokenCount(t), 1]) and an LLM-based interactivity score RLLM(t) ∈ [0, 1].
  
2. Bayesian Meta-Calibrator:
   • Train a lightweight meta-model (using Bayesian linear regression) to fuse the ensemble signals into a unified reward estimate and produce a robust uncertainty metric.
   • The output consists of an aggregated reward R*(t|g) and an uncertainty score, which will decide subsequent action.

3. Uncertainty-Guided Clarification Module:
   • Implement a dedicated clarification module built on a lightweight transformer.
   • This module receives the fused uncertainty score and relevant conversational context to dynamically decide: 
       – (a) to generate a clarifying question (disambiguation or request for additional details) when uncertainty is high, or 
       – (b) to produce a direct answer otherwise.
   • Bayesian calibration is applied to set dynamic thresholds for triggering clarifications.

4. Two-Phase Training Pipeline:
   Phase A – Pretraining:
       • Generate synthetic multiturn dialogues for the three domains (MediumDocEdit-Chat, BigCodeBench-Chat, MATH-Chat).
       • Pretrain both the ensemble reward predictors and the Bayesian meta-model on these dialogues.
       • Pretrain the clarification module on synthetic data annotated with optimal clarification actions.
   Phase B – Active Fine-Tuning:
       • Integrate the pretrained modules into Llama-3.1-8B (enhanced with LoRA, r=32, α=16, dropout=0.1).
       • Employ an active learning framework: when the Bayesian meta-model detects high uncertainty, trigger a clarification round.
       • Utilize corrective feedback from these rounds to update both the meta-calibrator’s aggregation weights and the clarification module’s policy.

5. Evaluation Metrics and Experimental Setup:
   • Task-Specific Metrics:
       - MediumDocEdit-Chat: BLEU score combined with LLM-judged narrative coherence; efficiency measured via token count.
       - BigCodeBench-Chat: Unit test pass rate plus static code analysis metrics.
       - MATH-Chat: Final answer accuracy augmented with a logical coherence score.
   • Clarification Efficiency Score:
       - New metric that quantifies the reduction in uncertainty per clarification round, balancing dialogue length and improved accuracy.
   • Baselines for Comparison:
       - Base (Llama-3.1-8B with no adaptive clarifications).
       - Proactive Base (LLM with fixed, prompt-engineered clarifications).
       - Fine-tuned variants using standard SFT, Offline DPO, Online DPO, and PPO (all without active clarification rounds).
   • Adversarial Challenge Rounds:
       - Periodically inject ambiguous or misleading prompts using an adversarial user simulator.
       - Perform ablation studies to assess sensitivity to noise and robustness of the ensemble and clarification system.
   • Additional Generalization Study:
       - Use an Abg-CoQA setup focusing on ambiguous vs. non-ambiguous items to examine ask vs. answer strategies.

Timeline & Deliverables:
   • Month 1–2: Develop and pretrain ensemble reward predictors and Bayesian meta-model on synthetic dialogues.
   • Month 3: Design and pretrain the clarification module; integrate all modules with Llama-3.1-8B (LoRA).
   • Month 4: Active fine-tuning with active learning and clarification rounds.
   • Month 5: Run full evaluations including adversarial challenge rounds and baseline comparisons.
   • Month 6: Analyze results, perform ablation studies, and compile findings.

This plan merges innovations from multiturn-aware rewards, ensemble modeling, and Bayesian calibration with dynamic clarification strategies to enhance human-LLM collaboration across diverse tasks.
    
Expected Outcomes:
   – Enhanced dialogue accuracy and efficiency across tasks.
   – Quantified benefits of dynamic clarification in reducing uncertainty.
   – Insights on robustness under adversarial conditions and effective ask-vs-answer strategies.

This ensemble-based, Bayesian-calibrated, uncertainty-guided approach offers a novel path to fully harness multiturn interactions, setting new benchmarks in collaborative LLM performance.
  
Good luck, and let’s proceed with implementation details in our next meeting!
  
— End of Plan
``` 
