Reviewer #2:
(5.568181818181816, 'The performance of your submission is: 5.568181818181816THOUGHT:\nThe paper proposes a framework that integrates ensemble Monte Carlo reward predictors with Bayesian meta-calibration and integrates an uncertainty-triggered clarification module to enhance multi-turn human–LLM collaboration. While the attempt to merge these components across diverse domains is interesting and ambitious, several concerns arise. The methodological description is overly complex and sometimes convoluted, and the experimental evaluation is mixed. In particular, the drop in performance in the code generation domain raises questions about the robustness of the approach. Moreover, reproducibility details and sensitivity analysis on hyperparameters (e.g., threshold τ and bonus δ) need further clarity. Although the paper has several strengths (a solid mathematical formulation, detailed experiments, and a unique idea to invoke clarification dynamically), the overall presentation suffers from clarity issues and insufficient justification for some design choices. Given these weaknesses, my decision is a Reject.\n\nREVIEW JSON:\n```json\n{\n  "Summary": "The paper introduces an ensemble-based reward estimation framework for multiturn human-LLM collaboration. It leverages multiple Monte Carlo reward predictors, which are aggregated using Bayesian linear regression to yield a unified reward estimate and an uncertainty score. When uncertainty exceeds a fixed threshold, an uncertainty-guided clarification module triggers additional rounds to ask for disambiguation. The approach is evaluated on several domains including document editing, code generation, math problem solving, and ambiguity resolution, with mixed results.",\n  "Strengths": [\n    "Innovative integration of ensemble-based reward predictors with Bayesian uncertainty estimation.",\n    "Introduction of a dynamic clarification mechanism to mitigate ambiguity in multi-turn dialogues.",\n    "Comprehensive experimental evaluation across multiple domains and detailed ablation studies.",\n    "Solid mathematical formulation that grounds the approach in established probabilistic methods."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "The overall presentation is rather dense and can be difficult to follow, with some sections lacking clarity.",\n    "Key hyperparameters (e.g., the uncertainty threshold τ and bonus adjustment δ) are fixed without sufficient sensitivity analysis or justification.",\n    "Mixed experimental outcomes: while some domains show improvements, the code generation domain suffers a performance drop.",\n    "Reproducibility aspects are weakly addressed, with limited discussion on sharing code, datasets, or detailed configuration settings.",\n    "The trade-offs between computational overhead and performance gains are not thoroughly explored."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 3,\n  "Quality": 3,\n  "Clarity": 3,\n  "Significance": 3,\n  "Questions": [\n    "Can the authors provide a more in-depth sensitivity analysis on the critical hyperparameters such as the clarification threshold (τ) and the bonus (δ)?",\n    "What are the computational costs associated with running an ensemble of Monte Carlo predictors, especially in real-time applications?",\n    "How does the system dynamically balance between improvement in task accuracy and potential delays due to additional clarification rounds?",\n    "Could the authors clarify the reasons behind the performance drop observed in the code generation domain, and how might this be addressed?"\n  ],\n  "Limitations": [\n    "The increased computational overhead due to multiple ensemble evaluations may hinder practical deployment.",\n    "The fixed hyperparameter choices might not generalize well, leading to mixed performance across different domains.",\n    "Potential delays caused by clarification rounds could affect real-time responsiveness, impacting user experience.",\n    "Reproducibility is limited by the lack of publicly available code and detailed configuration settings."\n  ],\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 3,\n  "Presentation": 3,\n  "Contribution": 3,\n  "Overall": 3,\n  "Confidence": 5,\n  "Decision": "Reject"\n}\n```', True), 
Reviewer #3:
(7.409090909090907, 'The performance of your submission is: 7.409090909090907THOUGHT:\nThe paper under review proposes a multi-module framework that blends ensemble Monte Carlo reward predictors with Bayesian meta-calibration to drive uncertainty-guided clarifications in multiturn human–LLM collaborations. I appreciate the detailed design of the system and the rigorous experimentation across multiple domains. The integration of dynamic clarification based on uncertainty estimates is novel and promising. However, I have concerns regarding some trade-offs in specific domains (e.g., the observed performance drop in code generation), the clarity of certain mathematical formulations, and the computational overhead of running multiple ensemble predictors in real-world settings. I also feel that some hyperparameter-sensitive parts (threshold and bonus settings) would benefit from more discussion and justification. Overall, the method is innovative, technically solid, and well-motivated but would benefit from additional analysis on domain-specific performance and computational efficiency.\n\nREVIEW JSON:\n```json\n{\n  "Summary": "The paper introduces a novel framework for multiturn human–LLM collaboration that integrates an ensemble of Monte Carlo-based reward predictors, Bayesian meta-calibration for aggregating reward signals, and an uncertainty-guided clarification module. The system dynamically triggers clarifications when the aggregated uncertainty exceeds a threshold, and is evaluated across multiple domains including document editing, code generation, math problem solving, and ambiguity resolution. Empirical results demonstrate improvements in dialogue quality and task performance, although some domains (e.g., code generation) show trade-offs.",\n  "Strengths": [\n    "Innovative combination of ensemble learning, Bayesian calibration, and dynamic clarification.",\n    "Multi-domain evaluation that shows the method’s potential across diverse tasks.",\n    "Detailed experimental setup with ablation studies and a thorough extended discussion on potential improvements.",\n    "Clear formulation of the reward function balancing extrinsic and intrinsic components."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "Reduction in performance observed in the code generation domain indicates potential trade-offs.",\n    "Some mathematical formulations and parameter choices (such as the uncertainty threshold and bonus adjustment) lack sufficient justification or sensitivity analysis.",\n    "High computational overhead may be a concern for real-time deployment given the ensemble approach.",\n    "The evaluation relies on synthetic dialogues; real-world validation is limited."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 4,\n  "Quality": 3,\n  "Clarity": 3,\n  "Significance": 3,\n  "Questions": [\n    "Could the authors elaborate on the sensitivity of the performance with respect to the chosen uncertainty threshold (0.15) and bonus (0.05)?",\n    "How does the framework perform on real-world multiturn dialogues beyond synthetic datasets?",\n    "What strategies might be adopted to mitigate the drop in the unit test pass rate observed in the code generation domain?",\n    "Can the authors provide more details on how the computational overhead is managed, especially in scenarios demanding real-time responses?"\n  ],\n  "Limitations": [\n    "The trade-offs between improving dialogue clarity through clarifications and maintaining performance in fast-synthesis domains (e.g., code generation) need further investigation.",\n    "The approach may be computationally expensive due to the use of multiple Monte Carlo sampling predictors and ensemble aggregation, impacting scalability.",\n    "Reliance on synthetic data for pretraining and evaluation could limit generalizability to real-world applications."\n  ],\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 3,\n  "Presentation": 3,\n  "Contribution": 3,\n  "Overall": 7,\n  "Confidence": 4,\n  "Decision": "Accept"\n}\n```', True)
